          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Roger Penrose</strong><br>\nRoger Penrose was born in Colchester, Essex, England, on August 8, 1931. Growing up in a scientifically inclined family, his father was a psychiatrist and geneticist, and his brother became a noted chess player. Roger was drawn to mathematics from a young age, displaying an aptitude for science and abstract thinking. He studied at University College School and Cambridge University, where he excelled in mathematics.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Contributions to Mathematics</strong><br>\nPenrose has made significant contributions to the field of mathematics. He is known for his work on tiling patterns, specifically Penrose tilings, which are mathematical patterns that can cover a surface completely without repeating. These tilings have intriguing properties and are related to the theory of computation and the phenomenon known as quasicrystals in physical chemistry.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Discoveries in Astrophysics</strong><br>\nIn astrophysics, Penrose is famous for his groundbreaking work on black holes. His 1965 paper, written in collaboration with Stephen Hawking, demonstrated that black holes are a direct consequence of Albert Einstein's general theory of relativity. This was a pivotal moment in the field, changing the way scientists understood these enigmatic features of space.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
