          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Non-periodic Tiling and Quasicrystals</strong><br>\nIn the 1970s, Penrose's work on non-periodic tiling took a new significance when it was found to be related to the atomic structure of quasicrystals. Before this discovery, crystals were thought to have only periodic structures. Penrose's tilings revealed new possibilities and sparked a reevaluation of the nature of crystal formation.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Awards and Recognition</strong><br>\nOver his career, Penrose has received numerous prestigious awards. He was knighted in 1994 for his services to science and won the 2020 Nobel Prize in Physics for his discovery that black hole formation is a robust prediction of the general theory of relativity, sharing the prize with Reinhard Genzel and Andrea Ghez.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Nature of Consciousness</strong><br>\nApart from his work in mathematics and physics, Penrose has delved into the complex subject of consciousness. He has proposed that classical and quantum physics might be involved in the understanding of human consciousness, challenging the prevailing views that consciousness is solely the result of computational processes.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
